<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Emmanuel III of Sardinia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1701–1773</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Emmanuel III was duke of Savoy and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from 1730, when his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> abdicated, until 1773. He fought in the Wars of the Polish and Austrian Succession, gaining Novara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortona">Tortona</a> in 1738 and further districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in 1748 after the victory at the Assietta. With his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-bogino">Giovanni Battista Bogino</a> he reformed the administration of his states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iii-of-sardinia.jpg" alt="Charles Emmanuel III of Sardinia" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maria Giovanna Clementi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clementi_-_Charles_Emmanuel_III_in_coronation_robes,_Palazzo_Madama_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
